.TH CTHREADCOMM 7 "2026-10-06" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Overview"
.SH NAME
cthreadcomm \- overview of the thread communication API
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This module moves data between threads and lets a thread wait until a
queue or a file descriptor is ready. It is made of five parts:

  ccol_circular_queue   A bounded queue. A send blocks while the queue
                        is full, which gives backpressure to a producer.
  ccol_dynamic_queue    An unbounded queue. A send never waits.
  ccol_channel          Two circular queues, one in each direction;
                        the identity of the calling thread selects
                        the queue.
  ccol_select()         A one-shot wait until one of several queues
                        or file descriptors is ready; ready items
                        share the calls fairly.
  ccol_event_loop       A persistent reactor that runs callbacks for
                        a set of queues and file descriptors that can
                        change.
.fi
.SS Messages and ownership
.nf
Every queue carries values of type c_message_t:

  typedef struct c_message_t {
      void   *data;
      size_t  size;
  } c_message_t;

The library copies no payload.  A send that succeeds hands the
ownership of data to the queue and sets the data field of the sender
to NULL.  A receive hands the ownership to the receiver, which must
free data.  A send that fails leaves the message as it is, so the
caller keeps the ownership of data.  A message with a NULL data and a
size of 0 is valid, and it is the usual end-of-stream sentinel.  Every
send refuses, as ccol_invalid_args, a NULL data with a size above 0
and a data that is not NULL with a size of 0.
.fi
.SS Blocking, try and timed calls
.nf
A plain send or receive blocks. A try_ call never blocks and returns
ccol_container_full or ccol_container_empty instead. A timed_ call
takes a uint64_t count of microseconds, measured on CLOCK_MONOTONIC, so
changing the wall clock doesn't make the wait longer or shorter. With a
timeout of 0 a timed call behaves exactly like its try_ variant and
returns that variant's result. A timeout too large for a deadline (such
as UINT64_MAX) gets the largest deadline there is, so the deadline
never wraps around into the past.
.fi
.SS Readiness only
.nf
ccol_select() and ccol_event_loop only say that an item is ready; they
never receive or send anything. Right after that the caller makes its
own non-blocking call:
  - For a queue: ccol_circq_try_recv_zc(), ccol_dynmq_try_recv_zc(),
    ccol_circq_try_send_zc() or ccol_dynmq_send_zc().
  - For a file descriptor: read(2), recv(2), write(2) or send(2).
As with POSIX select(2), another consumer or producer can take the
message or the space first, so that call must not block and you always
have to check its result.

An event loop registration can watch a queue. While the registration
has a wake pending, a send to that queue makes no system call, because
one dispatch serves every send that came before it started.
.fi
.SS Handles
.nf
Queues and channels are plain pointers, and their destroy macros set
the pointer to NULL. ccol_event_loop and ccol_event_reg are opaque
VALUE handles (uint64_t) and not pointers. Compare them with
CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_INVALID and CCOL_EVENT_REG_INVALID, which are both 0.
Such a handle is resolved before the object behind it is used, so a
stale handle is refused and never dereferenced.
.fi
.SS Process exit
.nf
Event loop dispatch uses two thread-specific keys, created once per
process; queues and channels need no key. If the process has no free
key left, creating a loop fails with CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_INVALID for the
rest of that process, while queues and channels keep working.

The library's process-exit destructors delete the keys and free the
library's bookkeeping for all loops and queues. When a queue or a loop
is still live at that point, its own destroy does that release later,
so an application destructor that runs after the library's destructors
can still destroy the objects it owns. Once the release has happened,
creating a queue, a channel or a loop fails cleanly.
.fi
.SS fork(2)
.nf
There are two supported ways to use fork(2) with this module:
  - Call fork(2) before you create a queue, a channel or a loop.
  - Create the object, then call fork(2) followed at once by exec(3).
A child that keeps running with no exec(3) while an object from before
the fork is live is not supported. The child never inherits a queue's
lock in the locked state, and a queue has no thread of its own, so a
queue from the parent stays usable in the child. An event loop from
the parent has no reactor threads in the child, though, so it
dispatches nothing there.
.fi
